CARDS = [
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "When would you not use Node.js?", "When the work is CPU-bound. The event loop's advantage is concurrency of waiting, not parallelism of computing — one thread runs all your JavaScript, and it cannot be preempted, so a heavy computation stalls every connection in the process. Image processing, large data transforms, cryptography at volume: those want worker threads, a separate service, or a language with real parallelism. Also anywhere you genuinely need shared mutable state across requests, which separate processes make awkward."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "Should you set CPU limits on your Kubernetes pods?", "For latency-sensitive services, requests without limits is defensible and increasingly common: requests guarantee a share under contention, while limits only add throttling and cannot make anything faster. The counter-argument is real — without limits a misbehaving container degrades its neighbours, and capacity planning gets harder. What matters is making the decision knowingly rather than copying a template, and knowing that throttling shows up as p99 spikes at moderate average CPU."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "Alpine or Debian slim for a Node image?", "Slim is the safer default. Alpine uses musl instead of glibc, so binaries are not interchangeable — prebuilt native modules often fall back to compiling from source — DNS resolution behaves differently and has produced intermittent failures in Kubernetes, the default thread stack is 128 KB rather than 8 MB, and musl's allocator is slower under multi-threaded allocation-heavy load. Alpine is worth it when image size genuinely matters and you have tested your dependency tree on it."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "cluster or worker_threads?", "Cluster for isolation and for using cores on request handling: separate processes, separate heaps, and a crash contained to one worker — but nothing shared, which is why you end up needing Redis. Worker threads for CPU-bound work inside one process: they share the process and can transfer buffers or share a SharedArrayBuffer, but each costs a full V8 isolate, so you want a pool created at boot, not one per request. And a V8-level crash in a worker still kills the process."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "Containers or VMs?", "Not a choice in practice — the standard shape is containers inside VMs. The VM boundary is hardware-enforced and separates tenants; the container boundary is kernel-enforced and separates your own workloads while giving you packaging and density. You reach for stronger isolation — Firecracker, Kata, gVisor — specifically when you run untrusted code, which is why AWS Lambda uses microVMs."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "synchronous_commit off — would you do it?", "It depends entirely on what the data is. Turning it off does not risk corruption; it risks losing the last fraction of a second of committed transactions on a power failure. For analytics ingestion, event logging or a metrics pipeline that is a reasonable trade for a large throughput gain. For payments or anything a user is told succeeded, no. And note group commit already amortises the fsync across concurrent transactions, so measure before assuming you need it."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "When is a distributed lock the wrong tool?", "Whenever correctness depends on it. A holder can be paused past its lease by a GC pause, CPU throttling or a network partition, after which two processes believe they hold it — and no timeout tuning fixes that. It is safe as a contention reducer. To make it a correctness guarantee you need fencing tokens that the storage layer rejects when stale, which most designs do not have. Often the honest answer is to make the operation idempotent instead."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "Monolith or microservices?", "Term for term it is the monolithic-versus-microkernel argument from 1992. A monolith is one address space with function calls: fast, simple to deploy, no fault containment. Microservices are separate processes exchanging messages: independently deployable, failures contained, and every internal call goes from about 20 nanoseconds to about 200 microseconds. The resolution is likely the same as the kernel's — mostly monolithic, with boundaries where isolation genuinely earns four orders of magnitude."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "Unix socket or TCP on localhost for a local service?", "Unix socket, when both ends are on the same machine. It skips the entire TCP stack: no handshake, no ephemeral port allocation, no TIME_WAIT accumulation, lower latency — and the service becomes unreachable from the network by construction, with filesystem permissions as access control. It also gives you verified peer credentials. The reason not to is that it does not survive splitting the services onto separate machines."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "PM2 in a container — yes or no?", "No, in almost every case. It adds a process, duplicates restart logic the orchestrator already has, and — the real problem — it obscures the exit code, so Kubernetes cannot see that your application crashed because PM2 is still alive and healthy. It also breaks signal propagation. One process per container, restart handled by the orchestrator. On a bare VM without an orchestrator it is more defensible, though systemd does the same job with better process tracking."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "Interrupts or polling?", "Interrupts win when events are rare, polling wins when events are dense. A network card taking a million packets a second spends all its time entering and leaving handlers — receive livelock — so Linux's NAPI switches to polling under load. The same trade decides WebSocket push versus HTTP polling, database triggers versus batch scans, and filesystem watching versus periodic scanning. Ask what the event rate is."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "Why did Chrome accept a 10% memory increase for Site Isolation?", "Because Spectre showed that separating sites in software inside one process — V8 isolates and contexts — is no defence against a hardware side channel that reads memory the process is allowed to have. Moving each site into its own operating system process makes the boundary the hardware one. It is a clean example of choosing a stronger, more expensive boundary when the threat model changes, and the same reasoning is why untrusted workloads get microVMs."),
("tradeoff", "Trade-off", "Should you raise UV_THREADPOOL_SIZE?", "Only for genuinely blocking work, and never as a response to a blocked event loop, which it does nothing for. For CPU-bound threadpool work — crypto, zlib — the correct bound is your core count, because more runnable threads than cores just adds context switching. For file I/O waiting on a slow device it can exceed cores. Tell them apart by whether the threads sit in R or D state."),
]
